A1. Memories of the dear departed spirits of 1931. ~ HANGOVERS
A9. Here's a fast one for you. ~ DASH
A13. Greaser. ~ OLEO
A14. The Bard's favorite fairy. ~ ARIEL
A16. The boid that gets somewhere in Brooklyn. ~ OILY
A17. This is a warner, brother. ~ OMEN
A18. This regimental cut-up had quite an edge on for the New Year. ~ SABRE
A19. Walked the dogs. ~ TROD
A20. Canoe turbines. ~ PADDLES
A22. The kind of girl that's never chased. ~ CHASTE
A24. The course of this runs smooth in France. ~ OISE
A25. Man without women. ~ STAG
A26. What girls do in old cars. ~ STALL
A29. This is on the house. ~ HOUSETOP
A33. When the day you spoke of yesterday that would fall the day before tomorrow, falls. ~ TODAY
A34. The only thing he had on his mind. ~ HAIR
A35. To "I'm sorry dear!" ~ RUE
A36. Gambling chances. ~ ODDS
A37. This entitles bearer to one admission into the House of Lords. ~ TITLE
A38. Fanciful Italian name for red ink. ~ WINE
A39. A lyric poem, written to assuage the feelings, as a landlady's. ~ ODE
A40. Instrument of torture (slang). ~ UKE
A41. This contains rubber and bounces. ~ CHECK
A42. These come high in Spain. ~ PYRENNES
A45. Swoops around the dance floor as if well-oiled. ~ GLIDES
A46. The last thing aviators like to do. ~ LAND
A47. A collection of heavenly sparklers (plur.) ~ ARAS
A48. Ex-stable. ~ GARAGE
A51. These get oiled up and go places. ~ TANKERS
A55. The last thing they expect you to do in the theatre. ~ EXIT
A56. Just a squirt. ~ LEMON
A59. This has banks in Canada. ~ ERIE
A60. A bitter thing crossword solvers must face. ~ ALOE
A61. Bum English. ~ SLANG
A62. A good suspender. ~ ROPE
A63. Dips. ~ RETS
A64. A fast moving proposition that is good for a quick turnover. ~ SPEEDSTER

D1. This goes around with the younger generation. ~ HOOP
D2. A soulful gal. ~ ALMA
D3. What college men can always be found in. ~ NEED
D4. Water wagons. ~ GONDOLAS
D5. Pushovers. ~ VASES
D6. To rubber around. ~ ERASE
D7. A ticklish proposition. ~ RIB
D8. A quick dash for an East Indian. ~ SER
D9. Get a man in this and he becomes a kid again. ~ DOTAGE
D10. What stuck-ups put on for dinner. ~ AIRS
D11. Penny robber. ~ SLOT
D12. Two-faced guy. ~ HYDE
D15. This lulls college men to sleep. ~ LECTURE
D21. A knockout decoration. ~ LILY
D23. Is well-heeled. ~ HAS
D25. A growing proposition. ~ SOIL
D26. What badly bent brokers walk around with. ~ STOOP
D27. This warms the inner man. ~ TODDY
D28. Summer. ~ ADDER
D29. No likes. ~ HATES
D30. Took a crack at. ~ TRIED
D31. This doesn't carry much weight. ~ OUNCE
D32. Uses a keyhole. ~ PEEKS
D34. Thumbed one's way around the map. ~ HIKED
D37. Balloon-sized macaroni (plur.) ~ TUNNELS
D38. Pork chops and spinach. ~ WHISKERS
D41. A collection of close Scotchmen. ~ CLAN
D43. Puffs Up. ~ ELATES
D44. A drag, a bone and a hank o' hair. ~ NAG
D45. The Galloping Ghost. ~ GRANGE
D47. To clean up your dirty work. ~ ATONE
D48. This is shifty and hails from Michigan. ~ GEAR
D49. This helps the wheels of industry go round. ~ AXLE
D50. A red picnic. ~ RIOT
D52. If you get this far you're head and shoulders intc eroticism. ~ EROT
D53. Something apples are when they become adults. ~ RIPE
D54. A fellow with an eye for future developments. ~ SEER
D57. Overhead rattlers ~ ELS
D58. Mug. ~ MAP


